The separation of components from a mixture to purify a given product is a central part of chemistry synthesis. The separation/purification step in any industrial processes is essential and it corresponds to well above $50\%$ of the total cost with around $10-15\%$ of the world’s energy use devoted to such steps. The alkane/alkene separation was identified recently as one of the “7 chemical separation to change the world”.[1] Propylene $(C_{3}H_{6})$ is with ethylene $(C_{2}H_{4})$ , the largest feedstock in petrochemical industries with a global production that exceeds 200 million tons per year, with these chemicals mostly used to produce polymer-grade and plastic products, particularly the widely utilized polypropylene.[2] However, propylene is produced by steam cracking in mixture with propane $(C_{3}H_{8})$ that needs to be removed. The $C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}$ separation is highly challenging owing to the similar molecular size and volatility of these species This is commonly achieved by a highly energy-intensive cryogenic distillation with the use of columns containing over 200 trays due to the close boiling points of $C_{3}H_{6}$ and $C_{3}H_{8}$ .[3]
Adsorbent- and membrane-based separation is a promising and environmentally friendly alternative to cryogenic distillation. The most common adsorbents envisaged so far include aluminum oxide, mesoporous silica, zeolites and more recently Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs).[4] This later family of tunable porous hybrid materials emerged as a promising candidate for the C3H6/C3H8 separation under moderate conditions.[5-6] Different strategies have been envisioned for a separation via thermodynamics, molecular sieving or kinetics.[5-6] Typically, the ultra-microporous fluorinated NbOFFIVE-1-Ni was revealed as an optimal candidate to achieve a full molecular exclusion of $C_{3}H_{6}$ from $C_{3}H_{8}$ .[7] Fe-MOF-74 incorporating Fe as open metal sites demonstrated excellent $C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}$ separation performances governed by the stronger interactions of propylene with the Fe sites.[5] A few other MOFs with the appropriate pore apertures were initially considered as alternative solutions for a kinetic separation of the two hydrocarbon species that is also of great interest to ensure a high permeation selectivity for the resulting membrane.[8-10] Zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) was revealed as a promising MOF for such a kinetics control of the separation.[11-14] Many approaches have been recently considered, i.e. linker exchange, metal substitution and configuration optimization to enhance the $C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}$ diffusion selectivity achieved by ZIF-8 derivatives.[11, 15-19] This clearly emphasizes that boosting the level of performances of ZIF-8 for such challenging separation is topical since any improvement in terms of selectivity is expected to lead to a significant impact from an economic standpoint. Here we propose an innovative concept with the use of an external mechanical pressure to control the aperture size fluctuations of ZIF-8 upon adsorption that are expected to dominate the kinetics-based separation of propylene over propane. The control of one parameter at a time, either the adsorption[20-21] or the application of a mechanical constraint[22-23] acting as an internal and external pressure on the MOFs has only been explored so far. Nonetheless, to date no study has explored the synergistic combination of pore architecture and adsorption to optimize highly challenging separation-based processes. This concept proposes a complementary route to the current strategy that usually calls for the costly and timeconsuming development of refined adsorbent materials.
Herein molecular simulations based on a validated flexible force field for the MOF framework demonstrate that the application of an external pressure of 1.0 GPa confers to the mechanically-constrained ZIF-8 a significant enhancement of the $C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}$ diffusion selectivity by almost 1 order of magnitude compared to the pristine material. The mechanism at the origin of this phenomenon is further revealed by scrutinizing the concomitant diffusivity of the hydrocarbons and the dynamic response of the ZIF-8 framework to the coupled mechanical and adsorption stimuli.
Isothermal-isobaric $(N P T)$ molecular dynamic (MD) simulations were carried out to explore the mechanical constraint on the hydrocarbon-loaded ZIF-8 structure and its impact on the $C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}$ diffusion selectivity. The flexibility of ZIF-8 was treated using our previously reported force field[24] to describe the intra- and inter-molecular interactions of the host framework. The reliability of both force field and current MD method was first demonstrated by the excellent agreement between the simulated and experimental structural and mechanical features of the empty ZIF-8 in terms of cell parameter (Table S1: sim. 16.960 $\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ vs exp. 16.986 Å[23]), 6-ringmember gate size (Table S2: sim. 3.343 $\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ vs exp. 3.379 Å [23])
and bulk modulus (Figure S2: sim. 8.20 GPa vs exp. 6.52 ${\mathsf{G P a}}^{[25]},$ ). As a further stage, the diffusivities of propylene and propane in ZIF-8 with the loading of 5 molecules/unit cell (corresponding roughly to the experimental half-saturation) were computed at 1 bar.[13] Since these two hydrocarbons showed slow diffusion rate, MD simulations were performed at high temperatures and their self-diffusivities $(D_{\mathsf{s}})$ at room temperature we assessed from the Arrhenius representation (see Figure S7).[13] The resulting $D_{s}$ for $C_{3}H_{6}$ and $C_{3}H_{8}$ are $1.97\times10^{-12}\ \mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{s}$ and $1.26\times10^{-13}\ \mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{s}$ , respectively at $300~\mathsf{K}$ These simulated data are within the same range of available calculated values.[26] Moreover, our simulated $D_{s}$ values are in excellent agreement with the corresponding experimental data for $C_{3}H_{6}$ while they slightly deviate for $C_{3}H_{8}$ .[27-28] It should be noted that the experimental concentration dependence of the diffusivities was based on the message provided by transition state theory, as resulting from simply the adsorption isotherms. This good accordance between experimental and simulated selfdiffusivities supports an overall reliability of our current model to capture the diffusivity of the two hydrocarbons in ZIF-8.
Figure 1(a) further shows that the gate size of the hydrocarbons loaded ZIF-8 continuously diminishes when the mechanical pressure increases, with a ${\sim}0.3\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ reduction being achieved at 1 GPa, the fluctuation of the gate size $(\pm0.1\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ remaining the same for the whole range of applied pressure (Figure S4). This emphasizes that the mechanical stimulus can finely tune the dimension of the pore aperture of this MOF. This level of mechanical-induced contraction is comparable to that previously reported $(\sim0.5\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ for the closed-gate (AP)- open-gate (HP) configurations switching driven by the host-guest squeezing in ZIF-8.[23] Typically, the hydrocarbons-loaded structures under 1 GPa show unit cell volumes of 4346.4 and 4388.0 for $5C_{3}H_{6}$ and C3H8 molecules/unit cell respectively which are slightly below the corresponding data recently reported for $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ and Ar- loaded ZIF-8-HP under similar mechanical pressure.[29] Moreover, the application of the mechanical pressure was revealed to modulate the shape of the ZIF-8 gate via the unsynchronized shift movement of linkers forming the gate (Figure S5). Temperature was shown to only slightly affect the ZIF-8 gate size (Figure S6). A careful analysis of the mechanical-induced evolution of the swing angle of the imidazolate linkers (Figure 1b) evidenced that the mechanicallyconstrained gate structures for both propane and propyleneloaded ZIF-8 correspond to a variant of the known AP and HP configurations associated with swing angles of ${\sim}9^{\circ}$ and ${\sim}42^{\circ}$ , respectively.[23, 30] This clearly demonstrates that the application of a mechanical pressure on the hydrocarbons-loaded ZIF-8 stabilizes a series of gate structures, characterized by a swing angle of the imidazolate linkers ranging from $12^{\circ}$ to $25^{\circ}$ in the domain of applied pressure [1 bar- 1 GPa]. This structural behavior is reminiscent to that previously reported for a gradual increase of ${\sf N}_{2}$ concentration in ZIF-8. [21]
The mechanical controlled shape and size of the ZIF-8 gate creates a slowing down of the diffusivities for the two hydrocarbons, as shown by a downward shift in the Arrhenius plots for both $C_{3}H_{6}$ and $C_{3}H_{8}$ (Figure 2a and S10-15). However, interestingly, the so-induced decrease of the diffusivity is more pronounced for $C_{3}H_{8}$ compared to $C_{3}H_{6}$ . This behavior was explained by a lower decrease of the residence time in the ZIF-8 cage for $C_{3}H_{6}$ (7-fold) compared to $C_{3}H_{8}$ (13-fold) when the mechanical pressure increases from 1 bar to 1 GPa. Similar trend was also noticed for the inter-cage jumping frequency for $C_{3}H_{8}$ and $C_{3}H_{6}$ that decreases by a factor of 12 and 6 respectively under the” same conditions.
The resulting $D_{s}$ values were employed to determine the $C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}$ kinetic separation ability of the mechanicalconstrained ZIF-8 at room temperature. Figure 2 evidences that the predicted $C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}$ diffusion selectivity of 16 for the unconstrained ZIF-8, consistent with the previously reported data computed via dynamically corrected transition state theory[26], gradually increases in the range of applied pressure [1 bar-0.8 GPa]. Remarkably, above this critical pressure, there is a sudden increase of this kinetic selectivity by $\sim1$ order of magnitude leading to a value of $\mathord{\sim}110$ under the application of an external pressure of 1 GPa. Interestingly, this trend is also maintained when the mixture is considered $(C_{3}H_{6}$ : $1.00\times10^{-13}$ $\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{s}$ and $C_{3}H_{8}$ : $7.21\times10^{-16}~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{s}$ ; $C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}$ selectivity $\sim140)$ . Such a mechanical-control separation is a step forward compared to the most common chemical decoration strategy previously envisaged for the same targeted kinetic separation in ZIF-8.[11, 15-19]
This significant enhancement of the $C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}$ kinetic separation is a direct consequence of the mechanical-control of the framework flexibility. Indeed, Figure 3 and Figure S14 demonstrate that the application of the mechanical pressure maintains a high amplitude of ZIF-8 linker swing motion, instead of rendering the framework more rigid. This swing motion governs the ZIF-8 gate size fluctuation (Figure S15) that still allows both $C_{3}H_{6}$ and $C_{3}H_{8}$ to enter the mechanical-reduced ZIF8 gate. However, a higher activation energy is associated with the diffusion of the hydrocarbons in the constrained ZIF-8 $(C_{3}H_{6}$ : $28.9\ \mathsf{k J\ m o l^{-1}}$ at 1 GPa vs $19.6~\mathsf{k J}~\mathsf{m o l}^{-1}$ at 1 bar; $C_{3}H_{8}$ : 41.3 kJ mol-1 at 1 GPa vs $27.0\ \mathsf{k J}\mathsf{m o l}^{-1}$ at 1 bar). This is attributed to the existence of the new gate structure configuration characterized by a relatively large swing angle $\cdot25^{\circ}$ at 1 GPa and $300~\mathsf{K})$ . Indeed, the higher swing angle, the harder the guest crossing of the gate.
To shed light on the kinetic separation process at the atomistic scale, a careful analysis of the MD simulations was required. The swing angle of the linkers when the hydrocarbons pass through the gate of ZIF-8 was first recorded along the MD trajectories and its most frequent value is plotted as a function of the mechanical pressure for both $C_{3}H_{6}$ and $C_{3}H_{8}$ (Figure 4). At 1 bar, the swing motion mainly corresponds to a flip mode of the linker for both hydrocarbons. When the mechanical pressure increases a higher swing angle value accompanied with a larger contribution of linker libration are required to allow the diffusion of the bulkiest $C_{3}H_{8}$ (Figure 4). Especially, NMR experiments recently demonstrated that the activation energy for the linker libration $(9\ \mathsf{k J}\ \mathsf{m o l}^{-1})$ is much higher than that for the linker flip (1.5 kJ mol-1).[32] This implies that a linker libration drivendiffusion is expected to be slower compared to the linker flipbased one. Indeed, more frequent linker libration motions for the constrained ZIF-8 in the presence of the bulkiest C3H8 results into a more pronounced slowing down of its diffusivity compared to $C_{3}H_{6}$ thus leading to a progressive increase of the $C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}$ diffusion selectivity in the range [1 bar-0.8 GPa]. Above $0.8\mathsf{G P a}$ , there is a drastic increase of both swing angle and linker libration contribution for both hydrocarbons. This trend more pronounced for $C_{3}H_{8}$ compared to $C_{3}H_{6}$ is at the origin of the significant enhancement of the $C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}$ diffusion selectivity in this range of mechanical pressure (Figure 2b).
In summary, we demonstrated that a synergistic combination of mechanical-control pore architecture and adsorption in flexible MOFs offers a unique opportunity to develop new concepts for the optimization of the most challenging current-separation based processes. As a proof of concept, the application of an external pressure up to 1 GPa on the flexible ZIF-8 was demonstrated to create a significant enhancement of its $C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}$ diffusion selectivity. This behavior was achieved by a mechanical-control of the gate size and of the ligand flip/libration modes of the imidazole linker upon adsorption. Besides the kinetics-driven separation discussed here, the mechanical-control of the pore size/shape of flexible MOFs can be expected to optimize other current separation processes implying molecular sieving and configurational entropy-driven phenomena. This will be further explored with complementary molecular simulations combined with the development of a lab-prototype that might act as a spring-board for future applications at a larger scale.
